From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 11:15:43 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-303] BABY: Re: The Procreation Instinct

Nick Szabo writes:
>Furthermore, "baby hunger" can be satiated by much more 
>immediate and accessible feedback, in the form of adopting and 
>caring for a cute pet or even a cuddly toy.  Thus "baby hunger" 
>can be short circuited, just as birth control short circuits 
>the connection between heterosexual desire and making babies.

I think Nick overestimates the degree of substitution possible.
My wife and I had two nice cats and birth control, but where her
biological clock went "bing", she *had* to have a baby.  Then two.

>Finally, I again refer folks to the demographic literature,
>which shows quite clearly and overwhelmingly the self-genocide
>phenomenon.  Every significant culture that has access to, doesn't
>discourage the use of, and is skilled in the use of effective
>birth control has substantially negative population growth rate,
>without exception, across a wide variety cultural origins and
>other cultural patterns.  Whatever "baby hunger" there might be 
>doesn't come anywhere close to making up for the impact of birth 
>control, or even returning net population growth rate in these 
>cultures back up to zero.

Actually I understood the big result to be the "demographic
transition", where rich countries have fewer kids, and rich folks
within any one (modern) culture also have fewer (legit) kids.   Does
birth control add much beyond wealth/education?  

I recall a study showing stanford prof wifes in the 1920s has low
birth rates via effective, if not modern, birth control.

Has any enterprising sociobiologist come up with a theory to explain
the demographic transition?  It seems clear to me that some behavior
heuristic that used to help genes has been tricked by the modern
environment into doing the oppisite.  But it also seems clear that
there is enough variation that within a few tens of generations at
most genes could catch on, assuming no other changes trick them again.

Robin Hanson

