From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 11:22:22 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-306] SCI: Many-Worlds FAQ

I wrote:
> Many-Worlds, in contrast, looks very different in the finite and
> infinite cases.  In the infinite case, almost all worlds have
> histories which vindicate quantum statistics. In the finite case,
> almost all worlds have histories which vindicate equi-probable
> statistics.

Michael Clive Price writes:
>No, I don't agree because to there is an ambiguity (related to how you
>choose your basis) in how you define and hence count the worlds.  To
>remove the counting ambiguity you have to put the worlds on an equal
>footing, somehow, in some sense.  The only way that is unambiguous (ie
>basis independent) is to break up the wavefunction into equally normed
>components.  Then they can be compared by counting and we find that the
>worlds in which statistics work out okay increasingly predominate as the
>number of statistical tests increases.

I thought the whole point of the decoherence literature was to find
objective ways (not equi-norm) to define worlds, to choose the basis.
I was assuming one of these approaches would be successful, and that
thermodynamics would ensure a finite basis.  Hence I explicitly
assumed objective and finite splits in my first post.

Robin Hanson

