From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 13:26:28 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-10-307] Argument by Analogy (Was: Reply to Challenged Uploaders)

Ed Regis:

> this subject comes under the heading of the "Other
> Minds" problem: How do we know there are other minds?  The classical answer
> is: by analogy.  The argument goes: (1) I know that I am conscious by
> personal experience.  (2) Other organisms that are substantially similar to
> me--in terms of behavior, physical, physiological, biological, brain, neural,
> and molecular-biological structure, etc., et al.--are probably substantially
> similar in other ways too, including their possessing consciousness.
>  Therefore (conclusion), other people possess consciousness.  (The Argument
> from Analogy.)
...
> So, if you ask me how I know that you and other people are conscious, my
> answer is: by the Argument from Analogy, with the extra-added attraction, the
> Sub-Argument from Neurophysiology.

Thank you for taking the time to compose this clear response.  It also
makes it possible to express a position on uploads -- If you ask me why
I (currently) think that a sufficiently-well-done upload would be
conscious, my answer is: by the Argument from Analogy.  To me
(and probably to other "supporters" of uploading) the analogy
between a very accurate functional model of my personal neurophysiology
and my actual neurophysiology is at least as compelling as the
analogy between a dog's neurophysiology and mine.

derek


