From: mnr@cs.cmu.edu
Date: Tue, 18 Oct 1994 14:19-EDT
Subject: [#94-10-310] Argument by Analogy

Ed,

You claim to have seen something in yourself, with your inner eye, that
is important to you, and you wish to verify its presence in others.  Fine.

I also thank you for the correction, you're quite right - behaviorism
isn't the only way to ascribe consciousness.  Looking for structures
that seem to underlie what we experience as consciousness is a fine
endeavor.  I hope you succeed, and that the results support the idea
that uploading will capture everything that's important about
consciousness.  I think that's a foregone conclusion if we can
create a copy that acts _exactly_ like me.

Has someone mentioned Dennett's "Consciousness Explained"?  He spends a lot
of time discussing just how well (or poorly) we are able to see with our
"inner eyes".  He also tries to sketch out the physiological bases of
consciousness as best we know them.  It's a long book, but worthwhile.

---

Where you start to lose me is when you express concern that a perfect
simulation of yourself will not be conscious.  There are two main
trains of thought I see here:
  1. Even an atom-by-atom copy of me might not be conscious.  I wouldn't
     walk through a Star Trek transporter.
  2. It's important to be made out of physical atoms, and a simulation isn't.

The first point is the religious nonsense I referred to.  The second is
open to debate, I suppose, although I'd be hard pressed to tell a happy
community of uploads, who are able to express exactly what their lives
mean to them, that they aren't conscious because they have no atoms.

-- Marc Ringuette (mnr@cs.cmu.edu)

